 the actual PRD, I can't pinpoint exactly which features are needed. Let me shift focus and identify which packages actually have source code versus which ones are still unstarted. Looking at the source_code entries, I can see implementations exist for testutils, sys, format, and several commands like cat, du, wc, sponge, ls, true, false, and version. But cmd/yes, cmd/basename, and the cmd package itself don't appear in the source_code list, meaning those are genuinely missing implementations.\n\nNow I'm checking which of these unimplemented packages have complete specifications. Most of them—yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, and seq—are still in pending status. The only one with a complete spec is cmd/ts in release 99.2, though that release is marked as deferred and ts already has code at the generation-merged tags anyway, just not on the working branch.\n\nSo I can't propose ts since 99.2 isn't in the allowed release range. Looking at what I can actually work with, all the releases that have complete specs already have code started, while the ones without code are missing their specifications entirely. But wait—rel04.1 for ls extended shows spec_complete status with a use case that hasn't been started yet.\n\nThe challenge is I'm limited to 4 PRD sub-requirements, and I need to figure out what those actually are. From examining the ls code, I can see it covers R1-R4 and R6-R7, with R5 split into two parts: color handling for file arguments and write error detection. The implementation looks fairly comprehensive for basic listing, but I need to determine what's missing for the extended use case. D is prd008-ls, and I don't have its full text.\n\nLet me reconsider whether the test coverage is complete for existing code. The ls_test.go has tests for: R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R6.2. But the implementation covers many more requirements. Are there missing test cases?\n\nActually wait - the code_status analysis has a \"gaps\" field that is empty: `gaps: []`. This suggests the a