e, and human-readable file sizes—these would likely map to new requirement groups or sub-requirements. Rather than trying to tackle something as complex as long format output, I should focus on a more manageable feature that could fit within reasonable scope and still provide meaningful functionality.\n\nLooking at the existing test and implementation coverage, I notice there are gaps in requirements R3 and R4 that might be worth exploring instead of adding new extended ls features.\n\nFor rel02.0, I'm seeing several use cases with pending specs but existing code, while others like basename and dirname have pending specs with no implementation yet. The constraint about not re-proposing work for existing packages limits what I can do with ls tests, so I should focus on what's actually missing.\n\nLooking at the earlier releases—00.0 through 01.3 all have code in place, making rel02.0 the next incomplete one. But here's the issue: yes, basename, and dirname don't have PRDs at all, only pending spec status. The architecture says humans own the specification phase, and the generation pipeline is restricted to cmd/ and pkg/ directories anyway, so I can't propose code tasks without those PRDs in place.\n\nLet me consider rel04.1 instead—the ls extended features. The spec is complete and prd008-ls exists, so the stitch agent can read it and implement from there. I just need to reference the requirement IDs generically since I don't know the exact ones, and the agent will work from the PRD itself.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the constraint requires anchoring tasks to specific requirement IDs in the title, so guessing wrong would make the task misleading. Let me look at the existing test coverage instead—for du, the tests cover R1.1 through R1.3 and R1.5, but the implementation includes additional features like R2.2 through R2.4 that aren't tested differentially, which suggests there's a real gap here.\n\nThe constraint says not to propose creating or reimplementing existing packages, but adding test coverage isn't reimplementation—it's extending what's already there. Though there's a complication: the use case shows spec_complete but codestatus is not started, which seems inconsistent with the code that already exists.\n\nLooking at the actual coverage gap, du_test.go only tests R1.1 through R1.5, while the implementation handles R2.2, R2.3, R2.4, R2.7, R4.1, and R4.2. A test-only task would make sense here since the code exists and just needs differential coverage. But I need to respect the 4 sub-requirement limit per task, so I'd have to scope this carefully. I'm confirming these four sub-requirements are indeed under R2, then drafting the task description for adding differential tests covering the du flags. Let me also verify this aligns with the P1 priority of focusing on the earliest incomplete release, since Rel 01.3 contains du and needs test coverage.\n\nNow I'm checking the test coverage across the other releases to make sure du is actually the highest priority gap. Looking at the existing tests in pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat, and cmd/sponge to see what's already covered.\n\nFor sponge specifically, the tests hit the main behaviors well even though the implementation covers more features than what's tested. Since both sponge and du have partial test coverage but du is the later release, I should focus on completing du's test coverage first. through mode (R4.1)\n- Write to file (R1.1, R2.1)\n- Overwrite existing (R1.1, R2.1)\n- Append mode (R3.1)\n- Soak before write (R1.1)\n- Large stdin (R1.1)\n\nThese are the core behaviors I need to verify. The untested aspects lean more toward edge cases and error handling.\n\nFor du, the untested features are the actual user-facing flags (-a, -s, -c, -d), which seem more critical to test.\n\nBut P1 says I should tackle the earliest incomplete release first. Rel 01.2 (sponge) comes before rel 01.3 (du), so if sponge's test coverage is incomplete, that's where I should focus