I'm reconsidering the architecture constraints—cmd/version is technically a cmd/ target, but testing it would require unit tests rather than the differential testing approach the project uses, which feels inconsistent with the overall strategy. So du tests it is.\n\nLet me verify the specific requirements I'd be covering: the task would anchor to prd009-du R2.2, R2.3, R2.4, R2.7, and I can check the du implementation to confirm these flags are actually implemented and testable.\n\nEach test would follow the existing pattern—create a directory tree, run du with the flag, and compare the output against gdu to verify the behavior matches. The `-s` test checks for a single summary line, `-a` verifies files appear alongside directories, `-d` confirms depth limiting works, and `-c` ensures the grand total appears at the end.\n\nI need to make sure my design decisions actually come from the existing codebase rather than inventing new approaches. Looking at the source, I can derive three concrete patterns: using the TestMain setup from du_test.go to build the Go binary and locate the reference gdu via exec.LookPath, reusing the buildDirTree helper for creating test directory structures, and applying testutils.RunDiffTests to compare stdout between binaries byte-for-byte.\n\nGenerating test specification... I need to add differential tests for four specific requirements: the -s flag to show only totals, the -a flag to include all files, the -d flag to limit depth levels, and the -c flag to append a grand total line.\n\nI'll follow the existing test structure in du_test.go by using buildDirTree for fixtures, testutils.RunDiffTests for comparisons, and t.Parallel() in each test function. The new tests will rely on the goBinary and refBinary variables that TestMain already sets up, and I'll reuse the buildDirTree helper to construct the necessary directory hierarchies. Continuing with the grand total flag test and ensuring all new tests use parallel execution with the shared directory fixture. I'm verifying the test suite passes completely with both existing and new test cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94919,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94919},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35aa649d-6b61-4512-a8c2-2df29089a2e6","uuid":"6c90d8d9-5ab5-4836-b011-61be87a09a66"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NLYsSz1dpgN4WPfYSS1Z3h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using AI, and verifies parity via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded), `docs/` (human-authored specs).\n\n**Current Implementation**: Source code exists for: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat, cmd/du, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge, cmd/ls, cmd/true, cmd/false, cmd/version. Code status fields in the roadmap are stale — the source code is the ground truth.\n\n**Priority Analysis**: Releases 00.0 (infrastructure), 01.1 (cat), 01.2 (sponge) have code and adequate test coverage. Release 01.3 (du) has code but partial test coverage — the implementation covers flags `-a` (R2.3), `-s` (R2.2), `-d` (R2.4), `-c` (R2.7) but the test file only covers R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5. These four user-facing flags are implemented but not verified by differential tests. Release 02.0 (yes, basename, dirname) has pending specs and cannot proceed. Therefore rel01.3 is the earliest incomplete release with actionable work.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/du: differential tests for prd009-du R2.2, R2.3, R2.4, R2.7\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (PRD for du -- flag requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - cmd/du/du_test.go (existing differential tests -- follow this pattern exactly)\n      - cmd/du/main.go (implementation of -a, -s, -d, -c flags to understand behavior under test)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (RunDiffTests API and DiffTest struct)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/du_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test functions for -s, -a, -d, -c flags\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Add differential test for prd009-du R2.2: du -s prints only a single total line per command-line argument, suppressing all subdirectory lines. Test with a multi-level directory tree to confirm subdirectory lines are absent.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Add differential test for prd009-du R2.3: du -a prints a size line for every file encountered during traversal, not just directories. Test with a directory tree containing files at multiple levels.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Add differential test for prd009-du R2.4: du -d N limits reported entries to at most N levels below the command-line argument. Test with -d 0 (argument total only) and -d 1 (one level of subdirectories).\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Add differential test for prd009-du R2.7: du -c appends a grand total line after all arguments are processed. Test with multiple directory arguments to verify the total line sums all arguments.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Follow the existing test structure in du_test.go: use buildDirTree for directory fixtures, testutils.RunDiffTests for differential comparison, t.Parallel() in each test function.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Each new test function must use the goBinary and refBinary package-level variables set by the existing TestMain. Do not modify TestMain.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Reuse the buildDirTree helper from du_test.go to create the standard two-level directory structure (root/file1.txt, root/sub/file2.txt, root/sub/deep/file3.txt) for all new tests. This provides at least two nesting levels to exercise depth-related flags.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases, comparing stdout byte-for-byte between the Go binary and the gdu reference binary. The existing TestMain already builds the Go binary and locates gdu via exec.LookPath.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For the -c grand total test, pass multiple directory arguments (e.g., sub and deep directories from buildDirTree) to verify the total line aggregates across all arguments.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"TestDuSummaryFlag function exists and passes: du -s on the buildDirTree directory produces exactly one output line per argument, matching gdu -s output byte-for-byte.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"TestDuAllFilesFlag function exists and passes: du -a on a directory tree produces output listing every file, matching gdu -a output byte-for-byte.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TestDuMaxDepthFlag function exists and passes: du -d 0 and du -d 1 on a multi-level tree produce identical output to gdu with the same flags.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TestDuGrandTotalFlag function exists and passes: du -c with multiple arguments produces a final total line matching gdu -c output.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"All new test functions call t.Parallel() and follow the naming convention TestDu<Feature> consistent with existing tests.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/du/ passes with all existing and new tests succeeding.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94919,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94919},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35aa649d-6b61-4512-a8c2-2df29089a2e6","uuid":"c345d4c4-85c6-46dd-975d-b00128b99a6e"}
